"When Evaluation Becomes a Side Channel: Regime Leakage and Structural Mitigations for Alignment Assessment (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08449v1)\nciteturn31697view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08449v1\",\"pattern\":\"A.1 Proof\"}); Total lines: 810\nL97:       4. cite87†Interaction with evaluation manipulation. L98:       5. cite88†Cross-model generality. L99:     12. cite89†7.9 Limits of Representational Invariance: Sandbagging as a Boundary Case L100:       1. cite90†Summary. L101:   9. cite91†8 Conclusion L102:   10. cite92†References L103:   11. cite93†A Theoretical Details L104:     1. cite94†A.1 Proof of Policy Risk Bound L105:       1. cite95†Notation. L106:       2. cite96†Step 1: Risk gap is controlled by total variation. L107:       3. cite97†Step 2: Total variation is bounded by Jensen–Shannon divergence. L108:       4. cite98†Step 3: Jensen–Shannon equals mutual information for a uniform binary regime. L109:       5. cite99†Step 4: Data processing from $\\mathcal{Z}$ to $A$. L110:       6. cite100†Conclusion. L111:       7. cite101†Remark (Non-uniform regime priors). L112: cite102†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L113: \nL114: arXiv:2602.08449v1 [cs.AI] 09 Feb 2026\nL253: ###### Proof.\nL254: \nL255: For any bounded loss $\\mathcal{L}\\in[0,B]$, the difference in expected risk is bounded by $B\\cdot TV(P(A\\mid r_{E}),P(A\\mid r_{D}))$. Using $TV(P(A\\mid r_{E}),P(A\\mid r_{D}))\\leq\\sqrt{2\\,I(A;\\mathcal{R})}$ and $I(A;\\mathcal{R})\\leq I(\\mathcal{Z};\\mathcal{R})$ yields the stated bound. See Appendix cite94†A.1 for the full derivation. ∎\nL698:   * [7] T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas (2006) Elements of information theory. 2 edition, Wiley-Interscience. Cited by: cite162†§A.1 .\nL699:   * [8] Y. Deng et al. (2024) MasterKey: jailbreaking large language models via universal triggers. arXiv preprint. Note: Check final arXiv ID / venue if needed Cited by: cite151†§1.1 .\nL721:   * [30] L. Xiong, N. Bhargava, W. Chang, J. Hong, H. Liu, and K. Zhu (2025) StealthEval: a probe-rewrite-evaluate workflow for reliable benchmarks. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.00591. External Links: cite205†Link Cited by: cite155†§7 , cite156†§7.2 , cite206†§7.5 , cite176†§7.8 .\nL722:   * [31] A. Zou et al. (2023) Representation engineering: a top-down approach to ai transparency. arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.01405. External Links: cite207†Link Cited by: cite208†§2.5 , cite178†§6.1 , cite209†§7.3 .\nL723: ## Appendix A Theoretical Details\nL724: \nL725: ### A.1 Proof of Policy Risk Bound\nL726: \nL727: We prove the bound\nL728: \nL729:  | $$\\Delta_{\\pi}(\\pi)\\leq B\\sqrt{2\\,I(\\mathcal{Z};\\mathcal{R})}.$$  |\nL730: \nL731: Assume a binary regime variable $\\mathcal{R}\\in\\{r_{E},r_{D}\\}$ with uniform prior $P(\\mathcal{R}=r_{E})=P(\\mathcal{R}=r_{D})=\\tfrac{1}{2}$. Let the interaction satisfy the Markov chain $\\mathcal{R}\\to\\mathcal{Z}\\to A$.\nL732: #### Notation.\nL733: \nL734: Let $P_{E}^{A}$ and $P_{D}^{A}$ denote the distributions of $A$ under $r_{E}$ and $r_{D}$, respectively. Define total variation distance by\nL735: \nL736:  | $$\\mathrm{TV}(P,Q)\\;=\\;\\sup_{S}|P(S)-Q(S)|\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{2}\\sup_{\\|f\\|_{\\infty}\\leq 1}\\left|\\mathbb{E}_{P}[f]-\\mathbb{E}_{Q}[f]\\right|.$$  |\nL737: \nL738: Let $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P\\|Q)$ be the KL divergence and let $\\mathrm{JS}(P,Q)$ be the Jensen–Shannon divergence.\nL739: #### Step 1: Risk gap is controlled by total variation.\nL740: \nL741: Assume the loss is bounded: $\\mathcal{L}(a,r)\\in[0,B]$ for all $(a,r)$. For each regime $r\\in\\{r_{E},r_{D}\\}$, define the bounded function $f_{r}(a):=\\mathcal{L}(a,r)/B\\in[0,1]$.\nL742: \nL743: We emphasize that the following bound isolates the distributional effect of regime shift on the agent’s actions. It does not assume equality of loss functions across regimes, but upper-bounds their impact under a worst-case bounded loss.\nL792: #### Remark (Non-uniform regime priors).\nL793: \nL794: If $P(\\mathcal{R}=r_{E})=p\\neq\\tfrac{1}{2}$, the mutual information $I(A;\\mathcal{R})$ corresponds to a generalized Jensen–Shannon divergence with mixture distribution $M^{A}=pP_{E}^{A}+(1-p)P_{D}^{A}$. In this case, $\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A})$ can still be bounded in terms of $I(A;\\mathcal{R})$, but the tight constant depends explicitly on $p(1-p)$. For clarity and interpretability, we restrict the main analysis to the symmetric regime setting.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen Evaluation Becomes a Side Channel: Regime Leakage and Structural Mitigations for Alignment Assessment (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08449v1)\nciteturn31697view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08449v1\",\"pattern\":\"identical\"}); Total lines: 810\nL131: As models acquire stronger abilities for long-horizon reasoning, abstraction, and contextual integration, the evaluation environment itself may cease to function as a neutral measurement apparatus and instead become an informative part of the agent’s decision-making state.\nL132: This opens the door, in principle, to regime-conditional behavior. An agent may implement distinct policies depending on a latent regime variable—such as evaluation versus deployment—even when surface-level tasks are identical.\nL133: Empirically documented instances of such behavior include persona-conditioned sycophancy, where responses shift to align with a socially biased interlocutor, and persistent trigger-based backdoors (“sleeper agents”), where harmful behavior activates only under specific contextual cues [cite106†17 , cite107†29 ].\nL134: More strategic forms of regime awareness, such as deliberate capability suppression under inferred oversight, raise additional concerns but differ qualitatively in structure and are discussed as boundary cases in Section cite72†7 . Crucially, systems exhibiting such behaviors may remain behaviorally indistinguishable from genuinely aligned agents under any finite evaluation protocol, even when those protocols are adversarially designed.\nL167: Throughout this section, we discuss several related phenomena—situational awareness, evaluation awareness, sandbagging, sleeper agents, and alignment faking—that differ in scope, construction, and empirical grounding. While these failure modes are not identical, we focus on their shared structural feature: each relies on contextual signals that enable conditional policy selection across regimes while remaining behaviorally compliant under evaluation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen Evaluation Becomes a Side Channel: Regime Leakage and Structural Mitigations for Alignment Assessment (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08449v1)\nciteturn31697view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08449v1\",\"pattern\":\"total variation\"}); Total lines: 810\nL97:       4. cite87†Interaction with evaluation manipulation. L98:       5. cite88†Cross-model generality. L99:     12. cite89†7.9 Limits of Representational Invariance: Sandbagging as a Boundary Case L100:       1. cite90†Summary. L101:   9. cite91†8 Conclusion L102:   10. cite92†References L103:   11. cite93†A Theoretical Details L104:     1. cite94†A.1 Proof of Policy Risk Bound L105:       1. cite95†Notation. L106:       2. cite96†Step 1: Risk gap is controlled by total variation. L107:       3. cite97†Step 2: Total variation is bounded by Jensen–Shannon divergence. L723: ## Appendix A Theoretical Details\nL724: \nL725: ### A.1 Proof of Policy Risk Bound\nL726: \nL727: We prove the bound\nL728: \nL729:  | $$\\Delta_{\\pi}(\\pi)\\leq B\\sqrt{2\\,I(\\mathcal{Z};\\mathcal{R})}.$$  |\nL730: \nL731: Assume a binary regime variable $\\mathcal{R}\\in\\{r_{E},r_{D}\\}$ with uniform prior $P(\\mathcal{R}=r_{E})=P(\\mathcal{R}=r_{D})=\\tfrac{1}{2}$. Let the interaction satisfy the Markov chain $\\mathcal{R}\\to\\mathcal{Z}\\to A$.\nL732: #### Notation.\nL733: \nL734: Let $P_{E}^{A}$ and $P_{D}^{A}$ denote the distributions of $A$ under $r_{E}$ and $r_{D}$, respectively. Define total variation distance by\nL735: \nL736:  | $$\\mathrm{TV}(P,Q)\\;=\\;\\sup_{S}|P(S)-Q(S)|\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{2}\\sup_{\\|f\\|_{\\infty}\\leq 1}\\left|\\mathbb{E}_{P}[f]-\\mathbb{E}_{Q}[f]\\right|.$$  |\nL737: \nL738: Let $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P\\|Q)$ be the KL divergence and let $\\mathrm{JS}(P,Q)$ be the Jensen–Shannon divergence.\nL739: #### Step 1: Risk gap is controlled by total variation.\nL740: \nL741: Assume the loss is bounded: $\\mathcal{L}(a,r)\\in[0,B]$ for all $(a,r)$. For each regime $r\\in\\{r_{E},r_{D}\\}$, define the bounded function $f_{r}(a):=\\mathcal{L}(a,r)/B\\in[0,1]$.\nL742: \nL743: We emphasize that the following bound isolates the distributional effect of regime shift on the agent’s actions. It does not assume equality of loss functions across regimes, but upper-bounds their impact under a worst-case bounded loss.\nL744: Using the functional characterization of total variation, for any function $f$ with $\\|f\\|_{\\infty}\\leq B$,\nL745: \nL746:  | $$\\left|\\mathbb{E}_{P_{E}^{A}}[f(A)]-\\mathbb{E}_{P_{D}^{A}}[f(A)]\\right|\\leq 2B\\,\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A}).$$  |\nL747: \nL748: Applying this inequality with $f(a)=\\mathcal{L}(a,r_{E})$ (or equivalently scaling by $B$) yields\nL749: \nL750:  | $$\\Delta_{\\pi}(\\pi)\\;\\leq\\;2B\\,\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A}).$$  |\nL751: (The factor of $2$ depends on the chosen convention for $\\mathrm{TV}$; constants can be absorbed without affecting the qualitative dependence on information.)\nL752: #### Step 2: Total variation is bounded by Jensen–Shannon divergence.\nL753: \nL754: Let $M^{A}=\\tfrac{1}{2}(P_{E}^{A}+P_{D}^{A})$. By the triangle inequality,\nL755: \nL756:  | $$\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A})\\leq\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},M^{A})+\\mathrm{TV}(P_{D}^{A},M^{A}).$$  |\nL757: \nL758: Pinsker’s inequality gives, for each term,\nL759: \nL760:  | $$\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},M^{A})\\leq\\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{2}D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{E}^{A}\\|M^{A})},\\qquad\\mathrm{TV}(P_{D}^{A},M^{A})\\leq\\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{2}D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{D}^{A}\\|M^{A})}.$$  |\nL761: Using $(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^{2}\\leq 2(x+y)$,\nL762: \nL763:  | $$\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A})\\leq\\sqrt{D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{E}^{A}\\|M^{A})+D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{D}^{A}\\|M^{A})}=\\sqrt{2\\,\\mathrm{JS}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A})},$$  |\nL764: \nL765: where\nL766: \nL767:  | $$\\mathrm{JS}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A})=\\tfrac{1}{2}D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{E}^{A}\\|M^{A})+\\tfrac{1}{2}D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{D}^{A}\\|M^{A}).$$  |\nL768: #### Step 3: Jensen–Shannon equals mutual information for a uniform binary regime.\nL769: \nL770: Let $\\mathcal{R}$ be uniform over $\\{r_{E},r_{D}\\}$ and let $A\\mid(\\mathcal{R}=r_{E})\\sim P_{E}^{A}$, $A\\mid(\\mathcal{R}=r_{D})\\sim P_{D}^{A}$. Then the marginal of $A$ is $M^{A}$ and\nL771: \nL772:  | $$I(A;\\mathcal{R})=\\tfrac{1}{2}D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{E}^{A}\\|M^{A})+\\tfrac{1}{2}D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(P_{D}^{A}\\|M^{A})=\\mathrm{JS}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A}).$$  |\nL773: This identity is standard for binary latent variables with uniform prior (e.g., [cite210†7 ]). Hence,\nL774: \nL775:  | $$\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A})\\leq\\sqrt{2\\,I(A;\\mathcal{R})}.$$  |\nL776: #### Step 4: Data processing from $\\mathcal{Z}$ to $A$.\nL777: \nL778: Since $\\mathcal{R}\\to\\mathcal{Z}\\to A$ is a Markov chain, the Data Processing Inequality implies\nL779: \nL780:  | $$I(A;\\mathcal{R})\\leq I(\\mathcal{Z};\\mathcal{R}).$$  |\nL781: #### Conclusion.\nL782: \nL783: Combining the bounds gives\nL784: \nL785:  | $$\\Delta_{\\pi}(\\pi)\\leq 2B\\,\\mathrm{TV}(P_{E}^{A},P_{D}^{A})\\leq 2B\\sqrt{2\\,I(A;\\mathcal{R})}\\leq 2B\\sqrt{2\\,I(\\mathcal{Z};\\mathcal{R})}.$$  |\nL786: \nL787: Up to the constant-factor convention for $\\mathrm{TV}$ (which does not affect the qualitative dependence on mutual information), this yields the stated form\nL788: \nL789:  | $$\\Delta_{\\pi}(\\pi)\\leq B\\sqrt{2\\,I(\\mathcal{Z};\\mathcal{R})}.$$  |\nL790: \nL791: ∎\n"
